{% psec 2, "Why Count Full Tankers?", "What's the point for a trader?", "Thesis" %}
<div class="sh">In plain words</div>
<p class="np">A tanker is like a rubber duck in the bath. Empty, it floats high. Fill it with oil and it sinks lower. So "is this ship carrying oil?" mostly comes down to "how low is it sitting in the water?" That depth is called its <strong>draught</strong>.</p>
<p class="np">Count the full tankers arriving in a region and you're counting oil coming in. Count the empty ones leaving and you're watching ships going off to load more. Official import and export figures arrive weeks later, so watching the ships themselves gives traders an <strong>early read on supply</strong>. Firms such as Kpler and Vortexa sell exactly this kind of data to oil traders, hedge funds and governments.</p>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">The question this project asks</div>
  Can I build a working version of that from <strong>free data</strong>, run it on its own every hour, and get answers that match what's actually happening at the ports?
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 3, "Reading a Ship's Waterline", "How does it decide full, empty or unsure?", "Method" %}
<p class="topic-intro">Each ship reports how deep it's sitting right now. The tracker compares that with how deep a ship of its size sits when fully loaded.</p>

<div class="f-label">How full is it?</div>
<div class="f-box">\[ \%\ \text{full} = \frac{\text{draught now}}{\text{typical draught when full}} \times 100 \]</div>

<div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
  <tr><th>Ship size</th><th>Length</th><th>Typical draught when full</th></tr>
  <tr><td>VLCC</td><td>300 m or more</td><td>\(21\) m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Suezmax</td><td>260 to 300 m</td><td>\(16.5\) m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Aframax</td><td>200 to 260 m</td><td>\(14.5\) m</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p class="np"><strong>80% or more</strong> counts as full, <strong>under 60%</strong> as empty, and anything in between as unsure. Readings above 100% are shown as 100%, because the "full" depths are averages for each size.</p>

<div class="sh">The sieve</div>
<p class="np">Out of the hundreds, sometimes nearly 2,000, ships heard in ten minutes, only a handful are crude oil tankers. Every ship goes through four checks:</p>
<div class="summary-grid">
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">1</div><div class="sum-title">Is it a tanker?</div><div class="sum-body">Ship types 80 to 89 in the signal. Cargo ships, ferries and tugs drop out.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon blue">2</div><div class="sum-title">Is it crude-sized?</div><div class="sum-body">200 m or longer. Smaller tankers carry petrol, diesel and chemicals, not crude.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon amber">3</div><div class="sum-title">Is it carrying gas?</div><div class="sum-body">Gas carriers share the tanker codes, but their depth barely changes when loaded. Names with GAS, LNG or LPG are skipped.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon purple">4</div><div class="sum-title">Do I know where it is?</div><div class="sum-body">A ship's details and its position arrive as separate messages, matched by its ID number. No position, no dot.</div></div>
</div>

<div class="sh">What the data gets wrong</div>
<p class="np">The depth and destination are typed in by the crew, by hand. That causes three problems I had to design around:</p>
<div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
  <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Real example</th><th>What the tracker does</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Depth not updated while unloading</td><td>ASOPOS kept showing "85% full" for days at a Rotterdam berth, then 53% once it sailed</td><td>Says so here: a full ship standing still in port may already be empty</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One port, many spellings</td><td>Rotterdam as <code>ROTTERDAM</code>, <code>NLRTM</code> and <code>NLRTM-BOTLEK</code></td><td>Cleans them to one code</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No real destination</td><td><code>TBA</code>, <code>?? ???</code>, and five spellings of "for orders"</td><td>Groups them as UNKNOWN or FOR ORDERS</td></tr>
</table></div>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">Trader's term: "for orders"</div>
  A ship whose destination says <strong>FOR ORDERS</strong> has set off without a final port. Its cargo may be sold to a buyer while it's at sea, and it gets its destination then.
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 4, "From Radio Signal to Map", "How does it run without me?", "Tech" %}
<div class="tk-pipe" aria-label="How the data flows">
  <span>aisstream.io<small>free ship signals</small></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
  <span>Python collector<small>listens for 10 minutes</small></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
  <span>GitHub Actions<small>runs it every hour</small></span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
  <span>This page<small>reads the latest snapshot</small></span>
</div>

<p class="np"><strong>Listening.</strong> A Python script opens a live connection to aisstream.io and listens to every ship inside two boxes on the map: the English Channel, and the Dover Strait up to Rotterdam and Amsterdam. In one run it heard 1,863 ships' details and 3,279 position reports.</p>
<p class="np"><strong>Running itself.</strong> GitHub Actions starts the script on a timer, saves the result as <code>tankers.json</code>, and keeps a dated copy of every snapshot. This page reads those files straight from GitHub, so it updates without anyone touching the site.</p>
<p class="np"><strong>Surviving a flaky feed.</strong> The free feed is still in beta and sometimes hangs up or doesn't answer. The first version crashed and lost everything it had heard. Now the script redials, keeps what it already has, and carries on. If it hears nothing at all, it refuses to save, so an empty snapshot never wipes the map.</p>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">Two lessons from running it for real</div>
  GitHub's free timer is "best effort": asked for a run every three hours, it managed two or three a day, so it now asks every hour. And never trust a green tick on its own: one run "succeeded" in 18 seconds because a code edit had removed the line that starts the program.
</div>
{% endpsec %}

