<div class="sh">Caught in the data</div>
<p class="np">Because the tracker keeps visiting the same stretch of sea, it can follow individual ships from one snapshot to the next. These three show the method working:</p>
<div class="tk-stories">
  <article class="tk-story">
    <h3>SOHO SQUARE unloads in Rotterdam</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>12 Nov</b>At Rotterdam, 14.5 m deep: 100% full</li>
      <li><b>13 Nov, morning</b>Still in port, now 8.0 m: 55%, empty. Destination changed to "TBA"</li>
      <li><b>13 Nov, night</b>Sailing out at 14.5 knots</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A complete delivery: arrive full, pump the oil out, leave light.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="tk-story">
    <h3>KRITI FUTURE delivers to Le Havre</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>11 Nov</b>Heading for Le Havre, 15.6 m deep: 95% full</li>
      <li><b>12 Nov</b>Back out in the Channel at 9.5 m: 58%, empty. Destination "Piraeus, for orders"</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The first tanker the tracker ever saw, and its whole visit in two snapshots.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="tk-story">
    <h3>ASOPOS and the stale draught</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>12 Nov</b>In the Channel at 11 knots, heading for Rotterdam: 83% full</li>
      <li><b>13 to 15 Nov</b>Arrives and ties up at Rotterdam, still showing 85%</li>
      <li><b>18 Nov</b>Leaving at 11 knots, 7.7 m deep: 53%, empty</li>
    </ol>
    <p>It was unloading the whole time. The crew only updated its depth when it sailed.</p>
  </article>
</div>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">The pattern so far</div>
  Full tankers are mostly heading <strong>into</strong> Rotterdam, Fawley and Le Havre, where Europe's refineries are. Empty ones are leaving: several for the Americas (Houston, Guyana) and many "for orders". That fits Europe buying crude from across the Atlantic, but a week is far too short to call it a trend. That's what the next step is for.
</div>
{% endpsec %}

